;; Controls of the guard of the test tool (see ../../tools/flsemantics.ml,
;; "The check of static names") on which the tool does not exit with the
;; status 0, so that the rules of the directory above, which take the
;; status 0 and no other, cannot hold them. run.sh prints, after what the
;; tool printed, the first line of its standard error and its status.
;; `dune promote` updates an X.expected. The rules are written out below.
;;
;; And controls of the rule that the guard asks, from OCaml sources: the
;; five static_*.ml. Of the term that the tool's Simplify makes of each, a
;; static binding stands in a handler that is not at toplevel by Simplify's
;; own rule for the term it is given (each source says which form it is,
;; with the label of the discrepancy). Under the rule of before decision D60
;; the tool said of each that the term after Simplify is not well-formed,
;; and exited with the status 1. Now the two tests pass on both terms of
;; each, and the tool goes on to the two runs and to the validation. X.fl
;; is the term that the compiler gives Simplify, as ../ocaml/from_ocaml.sh
;; makes it of X.ml, and a rule checks that it still makes it; X.expected
;; is what the tool prints of X.fl, with the status 0. These units are in
;; none of the tables and counts of ../ocaml/README.md.
;;
;; The directory scope/ holds T5b's controls of the scope of static names
;; in a handler (decision D60): texts that the check of static names must
;; refuse and texts that it must pass, with a rule, a run.sh and a table
;; (scope/README.md) of their own. None of them is run by a rule of this
;; file.
;;
;; static_in_arm: a static binding in the handler of one arm of a test,
;; mentioned in that handler only. The term passes the two tests of the
;; guard (the binding is outside loops and code, and the symbol is in
;; scope where it is mentioned), so the tool gives it to Simplify. Simplify
;; takes a static binding at the toplevel of a unit only, and stops with a
;; fatal error. The tool catches nothing: the error is the compiler's, with
;; the compiler's status, and the tool prints no line of its own, neither
;; "not checked" nor a verdict.
;;
;; t1_14_unit: the term after is read from a text (the option -after), and
;; Simplify is not run. t1_14_unit_after.fl is the term that the compiler's
;; Simplify makes of t1_14_unit.ml with -flambda2-match-in-match, at -O2 and
;; at -O3 (label T1-14), kept as the compiler prints it (-dfexpr-to): the
;; symbol of a block is mentioned in the handler of one arm of a test, and
;; the block is bound in the handler of the other arm, so the field of the
;; block that holds it is read before anything is written there. No rule
;; makes that text again. t1_14_unit.fl is the term that the compiler gives
;; Simplify of the same source, as ../ocaml/from_ocaml.sh makes it, and a
;; rule checks that it still makes it. The term after is well-formed, and
;; the check of static names refuses it: the tool says so and exits with
;; the status 1, with no verdict. The tool's own Simplify does not make that
;; term of this text, with those flags or without them, which is why the
;; term after is given.
;;
;; after_static_in_loop: the term after is read from a text here too
;; (after_static_in_loop_after.fl, written by hand: Simplify did not make
;; it). It binds a block in the handler of a loop, where the term before
;; binds it before the loop. The term after is not well-formed: the tool
;; says so, with the part of the test that refused it, and exits with the
;; status 1, with no verdict. No other unit of the tests has that line.
;;
;; after_with_round_trip: the two texts of after_static_in_loop given with
;; -round-trip and -after together. The round trip runs Simplify itself and
;; reads no term after, so the tool refuses the two options together, with
;; a fatal error, and checks nothing.
;;
;; scope_diagnosis: not a run of the tool. The program of
;; ../../tools/scope_diagnosis_control.ml asks the function that finds the
;; tool's words for what is out of scope ("a symbol", "a code id") of three
;; terms made by hand, one of which mentions a symbol with a number and no
;; name, as a prefix makes for a field of which a .cmx says nothing. With
;; -after, which the texts above use to give a term after that is out of
;; scope, no .cmx is read and there is no prefix, so none of them reaches
;; that case.
;;
;; And one control of what the option -after cannot show, on which the tool
;; exits with the status 0.
;;
;; after_same_text: one text is given as the term before and as the term
;; after, of a unit that binds the code of a function that it exports, and
;; the tool says "not validated". The two texts are read independently: the
;; reader of texts makes its own code ids, function slots and value slots
;; for each, and gives no correspondence between those of the two texts
;; merely because their printed names match; only a symbol is the same in
;; both, by its name. So the validation can say "not validated" even where
;; the same text is given twice, as here. The option is for controls of the
;; guard, as the two above: under it "not validated" is no evidence that
;; two terms differ in meaning. after_same_text.fl is the term that the
;; compiler gives Simplify of after_same_text.ml, as ../ocaml/from_ocaml.sh
;; makes it, and a rule checks that it still makes it. Given that text
;; alone, the tool runs Simplify and validates the unit:
;; after_same_text_alone.expected.

(rule
 (with-stdout-to
  static_in_arm.output
  (run
   sh
   %{dep:run.sh}
   %{dep:../../tools/flsemantics.exe}
   %{dep:static_in_arm.fl})))

(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (action
  (diff static_in_arm.expected static_in_arm.output)))

(rule
 (targets t1_14_unit.fl.made)
 (deps %{ocamlopt} %{dep:t1_14_unit.mli})
 (action
  (run
   sh
   %{dep:../ocaml/from_ocaml.sh}
   %{ocamlopt}
   %{dep:t1_14_unit.ml}
   t1_14_unit.fl.made)))

(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (action
  (diff t1_14_unit.fl t1_14_unit.fl.made)))

(rule
 (with-stdout-to
  t1_14_unit.output
  (run
   sh
   %{dep:run.sh}
   %{dep:../../tools/flsemantics.exe}
   -after
   %{dep:t1_14_unit_after.fl}
   %{dep:t1_14_unit.fl})))

(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (action
  (diff t1_14_unit.expected t1_14_unit.output)))

(rule
 (with-stdout-to
  after_static_in_loop.output
  (run
   sh
   %{dep:run.sh}
   %{dep:../../tools/flsemantics.exe}
   -after
   %{dep:after_static_in_loop_after.fl}
   %{dep:after_static_in_loop.fl})))

(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (action
  (diff after_static_in_loop.expected after_static_in_loop.output)))

(rule
 (with-stdout-to
  after_with_round_trip.output
  (run
   sh
   %{dep:run.sh}
   %{dep:../../tools/flsemantics.exe}
   -round-trip
   -after
   %{dep:after_static_in_loop_after.fl}
   %{dep:after_static_in_loop.fl})))

(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (action
  (diff after_with_round_trip.expected after_with_round_trip.output)))

(rule
 (with-stdout-to
  scope_diagnosis.output
  (run %{dep:../../tools/scope_diagnosis_control.exe})))

(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (action
  (diff scope_diagnosis.expected scope_diagnosis.output)))

